  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This dataset shows an early but real trace of scholarly uptake: it has been cited once in the literature and appears in two documented downstream reuse records. The record is otherwise quiet, with no captured downloads or public mentions, so the main story is that this genome resource has entered research use rather than broad community attention.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Genome - Abeoforma whisleri is a Figshare-hosted genome assembly and annotation dataset released by Multicellgenome Lab in 2017. The deposit provides multiple file types for analysis, including the assembly, gene annotation, predicted coding sequences, and predicted peptides, and the record itself warns that the assembly is highly fragmented. Its subject profile centers on genome analysis and computational biology, fitting a resource intended for downstream comparative and annotation work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is scholarly reuse. OpenAlex records one citation, and the citing literature identifies a 2025 eLife article on G-protein-coupled receptor diversity and evolution in close relatives of metazoa. In addition, the dataset is tracked as reused in two downstream records, including an article mention in Europe PMC and a formal dataset citation pathway in Crossref-linked metadata. Taken together, these signals indicate that the resource has supported later research use, even though the citation base remains small.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding attention record is sparse. There are no captured event-data mentions, no scite citation annotations, and no recorded views or downloads in the available DataCite metadata. That pattern suggests limited measurable outward attention in the sources collected here, but it does not undermine the evidence of actual research reuse already documented in citations and reuse tracking.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Stewardship and access information point to a stable, openly available repository context. The dataset is deposited in Figshare with persistent identification, and OpenAIRE records open instances of the resource. A WorldCat holdings search is also present as a teaching-adoption signal, though the digest does not provide evidence of course use or other instructional uptake beyond that catalog presence.</p>",
